## Why this program is AGPL-3.0

This is not a preference that can be relaxed by editing a field. Two pieces of
this repository are modifications of Immich, and Immich is AGPL-3.0:

1. **`immich/v3.3.5-desktop.patch`** edits 11 files of the Immich server
   (`server/src/…`): it replaces the Redis/BullMQ queue with a Postgres-backed
   one, drops the Redis socket.io adapter, decodes HEIC through FFmpeg when
   sharp cannot, tolerates missing geodata, and fixes two POSIX-only path
   assumptions that break on Windows. A patch that modifies AGPL source is
   itself AGPL-licensed.
2. **The `immich-plugin-core` plugin** that a build ships in
   `runtime/plugins/immich-plugin-core` is compiled from Immich's own
   `packages/plugin-core` — see the `fetchCorePlugin` step in
   `scripts/prepare-runtime-macos.mjs` and `scripts/build-core-plugin-windows.mjs`.
   It is a build product of AGPL source, not a separate program.

A built app additionally contains the Immich server compiled from the patched
source, Immich's web UI in `runtime/www`, and the plugin above. Immich is
Copyright (c) the Immich contributors, licensed AGPL-3.0 (see
<https://github.com/immich-app/immich>).

The shell around it — the Rust supervisor in `src-tauri/src`, the React
interface in `src`, the CLIP service in `ml` — is original work and could in
principle be licensed separately, since it talks to the server over loopback
HTTP rather than linking to it. It is licensed AGPL-3.0 here anyway, because the
two are conveyed as one installer and a single licence removes any argument
about what the combined work is. If that ever needs to change, the split has to
be made deliberately for the original code only: the patch and the plugin stay
AGPL-3.0 whatever happens.

## Changes to Immich

Immich v3.3.5 is modified. Every changed file and every hunk is visible in
`immich/v3.3.5-desktop.patch`; `immich/README.md` lists the files and the
reasons. The upstream revision they apply to is pinned in `scripts/immich-ref.mjs`,
which every assembler reads so a build and its source archive cannot disagree.

## Corresponding source

Immich's AGPL requires that anyone who receives a built app can also get the
source that produced it. To reconstruct it:

```sh
git clone --depth 1 --branch v3.3.5 https://github.com/immich-app/immich.git
cd immich && git apply /path/to/immich/v3.3.5-desktop.patch
```

then build with `scripts/prepare-runtime-macos.mjs` (macOS) — parking that the
Windows runtime assembler does not exist yet, as `SHARE.md` explains.

`scripts/package-core-source.mjs` does that for you. It writes
`release/immich-<ref>-desktop-source-<date>.tar.gz`, the whole patched tree with
`.git` and `node_modules` dropped, and a `.sha256` beside it. Attach both to the
release. A patch against a public tag plus a build script is convenient, but it
is not by itself the "Corresponding Source" that AGPL §6 asks you to provide.

## Third-party components in a build

The installer conveys third-party binaries under their own licences, some of
which are copyleft. The full inventory, with the obligations each one creates,
is in [docs/licensing.md](docs/licensing.md). In short:

| Component | Licence |
| --- | --- |
| Immich server + web UI + core plugin | AGPL-3.0 |
| FFmpeg / ffprobe | **GPL-3.0** (Windows: gyan.dev build; macOS: `ffmpeg-static`) |
| PostgreSQL 17 with pgvector | PostgreSQL Licence |
| Node.js 24 | MIT |
| CLIP model `Xenova/clip-vit-base-patch32` | MIT |

The FFmpeg builds are GPL, which `PLAN.md` originally intended to avoid. They
run as separate processes, so they do not change the licence of this program,
but a build has to carry FFmpeg's licence and a way to get FFmpeg's source.
